                    NOTES FOR GCC2 USAGE UNDER OS-9/68k                    ===================================The files---------     GNU/CMDS/cc2               C compiler     GNU/CMDS/cc2plus           C++ compiler     GNU/CMDS/cccp2             preprocessor for C & C++     GNU/CMDS/gcc2              compiler executive     GNU/CMDS/collect           global con-/de-structor collector     LIB/libgccXXX.l            GCC support library (for use with old clibn.l)     LIB/gclibXXX.l             mix of new IO library, libgcc and old clib.l     LIB/libgppXXX.l            C++ class library     LIB/libiostreamXXX.l       C++ stream library     DEFS/GCC2/*.h              varargs for use with gcc & gcc2     DEFS/GPP2/*                C++ include files     GNU/GCLIB/*                files to create the gclibXXX.l librariesThe cccp2 preprocessors's search order for include files--------------------------------------------------------     <-I from commandline>     <C_INCLUDE_PATH environment> or <GCCDEF environment>      (C   only)     <CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH environment> or <GPPDEF environment>  (C++ only)     <MWOS environment>/SRC/DEFS/GPP2        (only if C++ & if MWOS is defined)     <MWOS environment>/OS9/SRC/DEFS/GPP2    (only if C++ & if MWOS is defined)     /dd/DEFS/GPP2                           (C++ only)     <MWOS environment>/SRC/DEFS/GCC2        (only if MWOS is defined)     <MWOS environment>/SRC/DEFS             (only if MWOS is defined)     <MWOS environment>/OS9/SRC/DEFS/GCC2    (only if MWOS is defined)     <MWOS environment>/OS9/SRC/DEFS         (only if MWOS is defined)     /dd/DEFS/GCC2     /dd/DEFS   The environment variable GCCDEF is only used, if C_INCLUDE_PATH is not set.   If several directories are specified in these environment variables, they   must be separated by colons.   The '/dd' in the above paths can be replaced by the contents of the   environment variable OSK_DD. This can be important for cross compilers.The gcc2 compiler's search rules for libraries----------------------------------------------   If a library is specified using the '-l<name>' syntax, gcc2 will search   for the following files in the following order:     <dir>/<name>     <dir>/<name>.l     (only searched if <name> does not contain a dot '.')     <dir>/lib<name>.l  (only searched if <name> does not contain a dot '.')   <dir> is one of the following names (order of usage) :     -L<dir>            (in the same order as they appear in the command line)     <GCCLIB environment>     <MWOS environment>/OS9/68020/LIB   (only if -ucclibs & -m(c)680x0 x=2,3,4)     <MWOS environment>/OS9/68000/LIB   (only if -ucclibs)     /dd/LIB                            (only if not -ucclibs)     /dd/GNULIB                         (only if not -ucclibs)   If <name> is not found using the above rules, gcc2 passes -l=<name> to the   linker; otherwise, the complete path name of the library file is composed   and passed to the linker.   The '/dd' in the above paths can be replaced by the contents of the   environment variable OSK_DD. This can be important for cross compilers.The cccp2 preprocessors's predefined macros-------------------------------------------   Depending on the given options, different macros are predefined for cccp2.   Note that the value of the macro sometimes gives more information about a   specified option.        macro        value  condition     --------------- ----- -----------------------------------------------     __UWLIBS__        1    -uwlibs     __UCCLIBS__       1    -ucclibs     __UCCMODE__       1    -muccmode     __UCCMODE__       2    -muccmode2     __OPTIMIZE__      1    -O     __OPTIMIZE__      2    -O2     __GNUC__          2    always for C and C++     __GNUG__          2    only for C++     __cplusplus       1    only for C++     __STRICT_ANSI__   1    -ansi     __cpu32__         1    -mcpu32     __mc68020__       1    -m68020 or -mc68020 or -m68030 or -mc68030                            or -m68020-40 -mc68020-40     __mc68040__       1    -m68040 or -mc68040 or -mc68040-only or -m68040-only     __mc68060__       1    -m68060 or -mc68060     __mc68000__       1    -m68000 or -mc68000 or none of the cpu switches     mc68000           1    -m68000 or -mc68000 or none of the cpu switches     __mc68881__       1    -m68881     __CHAR_UNSIGNED__ 1    -funsigned-char     OSK               1    always     _OSK              1    always   cccp2 itself defines some more macros which are described in the   preprocessor documentation (GNU/DOC/cpp.texi).Environment Variables---------------------     C_INCLUDE_PATH      path for include files     GCCDEF              path for include files if C_INCLUDE_PATH is not used     CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH  path for include files     GPPDEF              path for include files if CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH is not used     GCCLIB              path for library search (paths separated by :)     TMPDIR              path for temporary files     GCC2_OPTS           list of gcc2's default options     MWOS                if defined cccp2 searches relative to defined path for                         include files, gcc2 seraches for libraries relative                         to the same path if used with -ucclibs     OSK_DD              specifies the root point of OSK's directory structure                         It's contents replaces the '/dd' in the search paths                         for include files and libraries.                         This variable should be set for cross compilers.Temporary files---------------   The gcc2 compiler uses the directory specified in the environment variable   TMPDIR for temporary files. This setting can be overridden by using the   -T<tmpdir> command line option. If neither is set, temporary files are   created in the current working directory.Sourcefile extensions---------------------   The gcc2 executive chooses the compiler by the sourcefile extension :   .c   -> cc2   .cpp -> cc2   .cc  -> cc2plus   .cxx -> cc2plus   .C   -> cc2plus   .a   -> r68   .o68 -> r68   .r   -> l68gcc2's Stackframe-----------------   Depending on the given options gcc2 uses different stackframes. Options that   influence the stackframe are :     -fomit-frame-pointer     -muse-argptr     -mdynamic-stack   Normally a stackframe looks like this :     stack-parameters     return address     old frame-pointer      <= frame-pointer (a5)     local variables     saved registers        <= stack-pointer (a7)   If -muse-argptr is used the stackframe changes as follows :     stack-parameters     return address     old arg-pointer        <= argument-pointer (a4)     old frame-pointer      <= frame-pointer (a5)     local variables     saved registers        <= stack-pointer (a7)   The option -mdynamic-stack can lead to the following :     stack-parameters     return address     old arg-pointer        <= argument-pointer (a4)    and somewhere else in memory to :     ptr to old stackframe     old stack-check info     size of new stack     stack return-addr     dummy arg-pointer     old frame-pointer      <= frame-pointer (a5)     local variables     saved registers        <= stack-pointer (a7)     local stack-expandRules for using gcc2 in the MWOS environment--------------------------------------------   If the gcc2 archives are unpacked, they appear in an old style directory   structure. To move the files to the right place in the MWOS directory do   the following :     move DEFS/GCC2  into MWOS/SRC/OS9/DEFS     move DEFS/GPP2  into MWOS/SRC/OS9/DEFS     move LIB/*000.l into MWOS/OS9/68000/LIB     move LIB/*020.l into MWOS/OS9/68020/LIB     move LIB/*881.l into MWOS/OS9/68020/LIB   Then add /dd/GNU/CMDS to the environment variable PATH.   If programs are compiled with -ucclibs and the the environment variable   MWOS is set, gcc2 follows the rules for include files and libraries as   described above. It is better not to add the MWOS/SRC/DEFS/GCC2 and   MWOS/SRC/DEFS/GPP2 paths to the GCCDEF (C_INCLUDE_PATH) or GPPDEF   (CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH) environment variables, unless the described order of   include paths (as described above) is NOT changed. Note also that for C++   programs the GCCDEF (C_INCLUDE_PATH) environment has NO effect, i.e. both   the C++ and C paths have to be added to GPPDEF (CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH).   The environment variable GCCLIB must not be set to a directory containing   standard libraries or a a standard cstart.r file.Trouble shooting----------------    Q : The cc2 compiler terminates with stack overflow.    A : Specify -Fcc2s200 (-Fcc2pluss200) or similar in order to increase        the compiler's stack size.    Q : The option -mlong-calls does not work for a 68000 target.    A : This option requires at least version 2.9 of the r68 assembler        (for proper operartion the -ucc and -ob options must also be given).    Q : A program compiled with gcc2 terminates with Error #000:106.    A : This is a stack overflow condition, if the program was compiled with        the -m68020 option. The gcc2 compiler uses an optimized technique        for stack checking (chk2 instruction). The -mstdstack-check option        can be specified to switch to the older (slower) stack checking code.        Some older versions of libgcc.l showed a similar problem, if the main        function used a lot of local variables.    Q : After installation of a new version of G++, old programs cannot be        linked anymore.    A : Internal symbol names may change from one version to another        (virtual function tables or mangled function names). New G++ versions        require, therefore, that an unconditional make (-u) is done at least        once.    Q : The linker complains about unresolved references when trying to link        C++ programs (-++lib).    A : Due to problems in l68 (old version) and l68/libgen (new versions),        some libraries have to be specified twice (libgppXXX.l) or even three        times (libiostreamXXX.l).    Q : Can I use the new C-libraries from UltraC?    A : YES, there is a gcc2 switch '-ucclibs' which links the new libraries.        It is necessary to compile the entire program with this switch because        Microware has slightly changed the rules for register usage from        CC V3.2 to UltraC.    Q : A program compiled with gcc2 only works properly when compiled        without optimization.    A : Some library functions in clibXXXX.l do not save all registers they        use (for example strtod). Please use a newer version of the library.    Q : A program that contains inline assembly code can only be compiled with        or without the -ucc option, because of the required register prefix.    A : There is a macro in the preprocessor that returns the register prefix.        Write for example :          __asm__("move.l "__REGISTER_PREFIX__"d0,-("__REGISTER_PREFIX__"a7)");        or          #define R __REGISTER_PREFIX__          __asm__("move.l "R"d0,-("R"a7)");        which is much shorter if you have more than one __asm statement.        If the inline assembler code is inside a function an easier way can        used, just write '%/' before each register name. This will be changed        to the appropriate prefix :          void foo(void *param)          {            __asm__("move.l %0,%/a3" : : "g" (param) : "a3");            __asm__("jsr (%/a3)" : : : "a3");          }        To notify the compiler of the usage of the register d1 the used        registers should be specified in the third section of parameters.    Q : Can .r files compiled with old stdio.h be linked with new .r files ?    A : YES, the new I/O system contains code for compatibility with the old        stdio.    Q : How can I use gcc2 with UltraC libraries ?    A : the MWOS environment variable must be defined AND the -ucclibs option        must be given to gcc2.    Q : Can I make libraries that suit for all available C        compilers (CC V3.2, UltraC, gcc and gcc2) ?    A : YES, the modules must be compiled with -muccmode2. This forces all        used registers to be saved but does not rely on other functions to        do the same (this is important for functions that take function-        pointers as arguments, e.g. qsort() ).Special OSK options-------------------  -mremote           By default GCC generates 16-bit offsets to variables.                     For programs declating more than 64k bytes of valiables,                     this option is required.  -mlong-calls       By default GCC generates 16-bit code offsets. They can be                     extended to 32-bits with this options. If this option is                     used for a 68000 target, the -ucc option must also be                     given.  -mnostack-check    This option turns the stack-checking code off, which                     results in faster code.  -mstdstack-check   GCC normally uses a faster technique for stack-checking on                     68020/030/040 processors (not for 68060!).                     This can be turned off with this option.  -mnocom            In C language it is possible to declare in two modules                     twice the same variable without using the extern prefix. To                     implement this variables have to be mapped into the common                     section instead of the standard vsect. All known versions                     of l68 run into problems if one of those varaibles is                     initialized. Therefore this feature can be turned off using                     this option.  -mgss              If a C programm has to be debugged on assembly level                     (for example drivers) is is useful to have global labels                     for static functions, which can be enabled with this                     option. The lables will be of the form <function>@<module>.  -++lib             Adds the standard C++ libraries to the linklist.  -uwlibs            Link the orignal clib instead of gclibXXX.l.                     This options can not be use together with -++lib.  -ucclibs           Link UltraC libraries instead of gclibXXX.l.  -muccmode          Compile for use as an UltraC library. This switch changes                     the register-usage, d1,a0,a1,fp0 and fp1 are nolonger                     scratch registers. This switch is also active if -ucclibs                     is specified.  -muccmode2         This switch is similar to -muccmode, but programs do not                     require that called functions really same the menioned                     registers. But functions compiled with this switch save                     all those registers. This switch can be used to compile                     a library for use with cc, UltraC or gcc.  -muccasm           Compile into assemblysource suitable for Microware's                     assembler delivered together with UltraC. This option                     forces gcc2 to insert a % before each registername to                     distinguiish between registers an symbols. Without this                     option eahc symbol is preceeded by a '=' for the same                     reason.  -muse-argptr       Compile programs to refer to stack arguments through a                     argument pointer instead of the frame- or stack-pointer.  -mdynamic-stack    Allow functions to dynamically expand their stack if                     required.  -col               The collector output file is not deleted.  -nocol             Force collect to output just an empty table instead of                     processing all specified files. This leads to fater                     link-times, but prevents C++ programs from working                     properly.  -optasm            Add a assembley optimizer pass between compiler and                     assembler. The -ucc switch decides wether to use o68 or                     opt68k.  -o68               Same function as -optasm which should be used instead.                     This switch is likely to be removed in one of the following                     versions of gcc.  -ctrap             Link trap library instead of full library. This switch must                     be used together with -uwlibs or -ucclibs. With -uwlibs it                     links the program to use the cio traphandler, with -ucclibs                     the csl traphandler is used.                     This switch will also support the future gsl (gclib-trap)  -cio               Same function as -ctrap which should be used instead.                     This switch is likely to be removed in one of the following                     versions of gcc.  -gsrcdbg           Generate a .dbg file for Microware's sourcelevel debugger.  -gg                Generate debugging info for the default sourcelevel                     debugger (currently srcdbg).  -g                 Generate .stb module for use with debug.  -F<prog>s<stack>p<prior>x[<xprog>]o[<opts>]                     Call <prog> with additional <stack>, priority <prior> and                     options <opts>                     -Fl68s100p200o[-sm] will result in 'l68 #100 ^200 -sm'                     For a Cross Compiler the x option can be used to choose                     an alternate <xprog> instead of the given <prog>                     to enclose the names for xname an opts '[' and ']' have                     to be used.-------------------------------------------------------------------------------         NOTES FOR GCC V2.x.x compilation under OS-9/68k         =============================================== - the script 'make_all' creates all necessary directories and then compiles   all three stages of gcc V2.x.x. For the first stage you need gcc V1.42   (cc1 Ed #30 needed to avoid 'illegal % ...' messages).   For a 'all stages' compilation of gcc2 you need the following sources   installed in your system :     /dd/GNU/GCC_2.x.x     /dd/GNU/GCLIB     /dd/GNU/LIBGPP_2.x.x - use gcc Ed #12 or remove '-A /dd/lib/cstart.r' from the makefile. - you also need a UNIX compatibility library like os9lib (including its   header files); the directory where these include files are expected   is specified as -I/dd/defs/os9lib in the makefile (please change if   necessary). - if you want to use 'make' directly you have to set the macro 'srcdir' in   the makefile. - if your computer runs out of memory, omit the -ob option from the OPT   macro [makefile]. - '-fbytecode' always results in a buserror. turn it off in tm.h until I   know the underlying problem of this error (not yet documented). - cc2plus seems to work fine, cc2obj has not yet been tested (93/12/06)PLEASE SEND ALL CHANGES MADE TO THE GCC SOURCES TO ME TO ENSURE THAT THEYARE INCLUDED IN THE NEXT RELEASE. E-mail to 'stp@effo.ch' or write toStephan Paschedag, Webermuehle 43/334, CH-5432 Neuenhof, Switzerland.